  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so the most critical unknown is whether multimodal fusion (combining RNA + image) actually provides a meaningful performance gain over a strong unimodal baseline. Without this reference, all multimodal results are uninterpretable. 2) What this tests: config_013 is the simplest possible image-only model (imagenet_vit_l, no aggregation, no fusion, singleprofile). It establishes a clean performance floor for the image modality alone. Any future multimodal configuration that fails to beat this baseline would indicate that the added complexity of RNA fusion is not justified. 3) Why best under limited budget: Starting with the strongest unimodal baseline (ViT-L is the most capable image encoder available) maximizes the information gained per experiment—if multimodal configs must beat a strong baseline, we avoid wasting budget on multimodal variants that merely match what a single modality already achieves. This single result also calibrates expectations for all subsequent comparisons (e.g., rna_only baselines, aggregation/fusion ablations) and directly addresses the principle of not assuming complexity yields gains."
}

